1 TITL Marquis
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Joseph-Jules-Paul-Marie-François de Cadoine, Marquis de Gabriac, born August 14, 1830, in Berne and died on November 23, 1903, in Paris, is a French diplomat.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Biography'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of Ernest de Cadoine de Gabriac, ambassador of Charles-Philippe of France, under Abbot Dupanloup, then at the School of Administration. He began as an attaché to the central administration at the Ministry of Foreign Affairs 4,
2 CONC  then attached to the archives, the political direction, and the cabinet of Drouyn de Lhuys and Count Walewski.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1857, in Austria, he married Baroness Mathilde von Eskeles, granddaughter of Baron Bernhard von Eskeles and Baron Karl von Brentano-Cimaroli. They had several children, including Arthur de Gabriac (1867-1948), music lover and p
2 CONC atron, married to Fanny Fithian (granddaughter of Richard B. Connolly and sister-in-law of Chester Alan Arthur II ).
2 CONT
2 CONT He was business attaché in Berlin, secretary in Naples in June 1859, in Rome in December 1859and in Munich in January 1862. He was first secretary of the embassy in Saint Petersburg from 1866 to 1870. At the fall of the Second Emp
2 CONC ire, he served as charge d'affaires, at the request of Jules Favre and the Count of Chaudordy and following the resignation of Ambassador General Fleury, maintaining relations between France and Russia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Following the signing of the Treaty of Frankfurt in 1871, ending the war between France and Germany, he was appointed charge d'affaires in Berlin to re-establish diplomatic relations between the two countries. His mission ended in
2 CONC  1872 with the appointment of Élie de Gontaut-Biron.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was ambassador to the Netherlands from 1872 to 1873, minister plenipotentiary of the first class in Athens in 1873, replacing Jules Ferry, and ambassador to Belgium from 1876 to 1878.
2 CONT
2 CONT Appointed French ambassador to the Holy See in 1878, he established excellent relations with Pope Leo XIII. Gabriac obtains from the sovereign pontiff that France obtains seven cardinals' hats, known as crown hats, instead of the 
2 CONC six that it had then. He left his post in 1880 following decrees signed by the French government against religious congregations.
2 CONT
2 CONT From then on, he remained outside of public life, contenting himself with writing articles in the Revue des deux Mondes and in Le Correspondant.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is Commander of the Legion of Honor, Grand Cross of the Order of the Dutch Lion, the Order of Leopold, the Order of the Savior, and the Order of Pius IX.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is buried in the Montmartre cemetery in a chapel where many members of his family such as his father and son rest.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT * The Church and the State: the maintenance of the Concordat in the interest of the State, the consequences of the separation of Church and State: text of the Concordat and supporting documents, 1886
2 CONT * Memories of an embassy secretary in Naples from 1811 to 1814: excursion to the principality of Benevento, 1891
2 CONT * Diplomatic memories of Russia and Germany (1870-1872), 1896
2 CONT * Memories of an embassy to the page Léon XIII (1878-1880), 1901
2 CONT * Notes and references 
2 CONT * "Grand Larousse encyclopedique, Volume 5" (p. 321), Librarire Larousse - 1960
2 CONT * “New illustrated Larousse” ( p.  241), Librairie Larousse – 1898
2 CONT * "National Dictionary of Contemporaries: containing notices from members of the Institut de France, the French government and parliament, the Academy of Medicine... Volume 3, edited by C.-E . Curinier ( p.  65)", Office général d
2 CONC 'éd. bookstore and printing. (Paris) - 1899-1919
2 CONT * "Permanent representatives of France in Turkey (1536-1991) and of Turkey in France (1797-1991)", Éditions Isis - 1991
2 CONT * The Eskeles Genealogy, 1995
2 CONT * Yves Marchasson, " Roman Diplomacy and the French Republic: in search of a conciliation 1879-1880", Éditions Beauchesne, 1974
