1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT ==Links:==
2 CONT *[http://geneall.net/en/name/6177/ferdinand-i-king-of-the-romanians/ Geneall]
2 CONT *[http://www.thepeerage.com/p10096.htm#i100956 The Peerage]
2 CONT *'''Wikipedia:''' [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ferdinand_I_of_Romania English ] [http://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ferdinand_I._(Rum%C3%A4nien)_ Deutsch ] [http://ro.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ferdinand_I_al_Rom%C3%A2niei Română]
2 CONT
2 CONT *'''King of Romania:''' Reign 10 October 1914 – 20 July 1927 , Coronation 15 October 1922 
2 CONT >'''Predecessor:''' [http://www.geni.com/people/Carol-I-Rege-al-Rom%C3%A2niei-King-of-the-Romanians/6000000002130133237 Carol I] '''Successor:''' [http://www.geni.com/people/Mihai-I-of-Romania/6000000003204158413 Michael I] 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Sepultura: Castelo de Peleş.
2 CONT
2 CONT Treaty of Bucharest: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Treaty_of_Bucharest_(1916)
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Ferdinand I, nicknamed Întregitorul ("the Unifier"), was King of Romania from 10 October 1914 until his death in 1927.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in Sigmaringen in southwestern Germany, the Roman Catholic Prince Ferdinand Viktor Albert Meinrad of Hohenzollern-Sigmaringen. The name was later shortened simply to Hohenzollern after the extinction of the Hohenzollern-Hechi
2 CONC ngen branch in 1869. The princes of Hohenzollern-Sigmaringen had ruled the principality until 1850, when it was annexed to Prussia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferdinand I was the son of Leopold, Prince of Hohenzollern-Sigmaringen, and Infanta Antónia of Portugal (1845–1913), daughter of Queen Maria II of Portugal and Prince Ferdinand of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha, heir to the Slovakian-origi
2 CONC nated Hungarian magnates of Kohary on his mother's side.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Following the renunciations, first of his father in 1880 and then of his elder brother Prince Wilhelm of Hohenzollern-Sigmaringen in 1886, young Ferdinand became the heir-presumptive to the throne of his childless uncle, King Caro
2 CONC l I of Romania, who would reign until his death in October 1914.[2] In 1889, the Romanian parliament recognized Ferdinand as a prince of Romania. The Romanian government did not require his conversion to Eastern Orthodoxy from Cat
2 CONC holicism, as was the common practice prior to this date, thus allowing him to continue with his born creed, but it was required that his children be raised Orthodox, the state religion of Romania. For agreeing to this, Ferdinand w
2 CONC as excommunicated from the Catholic Church, although this was later lifted.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferdinand's mother's first cousin Tsar Ferdinand I of Bulgaria sat on the throne of the neighbouring Bulgaria beginning in 1887, and was to become the greatest opponent of the kingdom of his Romanian cousins. The neighboring Emper
2 CONC or Francis Joseph, monarch of Austria-Hungary and as such, ruler of Transylvania, was Ferdinand's grandmother's first cousin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferdinand, a complete stranger in his new home, started to get close to one of Queen Elisabeth's ladies in waiting, Elena Văcărescu. Elisabeth, the Queen consort of Romania, very close to Elena herself, encouraged the romance, alt
2 CONC hough she was perfectly aware of the fact that a marriage between the two was forbidden by the Romanian constitution (according to the 1866 Constitution of Romania, the heir-presumptive to the throne was not allowed to marry a Rom
2 CONC anian).
2 CONT
2 CONT The affair caused a sort of dynastic crisis, in 1891. The result of this was the exile of both Elisabeth (in Neuwied) and Elena (in Paris), as well as a trip by Ferdinand through Europe in search of a suitable bride, whom he event
2 CONC ually found in Queen Victoria's granddaughter, Princess Marie of Edinburgh.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Sigmaringen on 10 January 1893, Prince Ferdinand of Romania married his distant cousin, the Lutheran Princess Marie of Edinburgh, daughter of Anglican Prince Alfred, Duke of Edinburgh and the Orthodox Grand Duchess Marie Alexan
2 CONC drovna of Russia. Marie and Ferdinand were third cousins in descent from Franz Frederick Anton, Duke of Saxe-Coburg-Saalfeld. Marie's paternal grandparents were Victoria of the United Kingdom and Prince Albert of Saxe-Coburg-Gotha
2 CONC . Her maternal grandparents were Alexander II of Russia and Marie of Hesse and by Rhine. The reigning Emperor of the neighbouring Russia, at the time of the marriage was Marie's uncle, Tsar Alexander III, who would be succeeded by
2 CONC  his eldest son, Marie's cousin, Tsar Nicholas II, the following year.
2 CONT
2 CONT The marriage produced 3 sons: Carol, Nicholas and Mircea (one of whom, Mircea, died in infancy) and 3 daughters: Elisabeta, Maria (Mignon) and Ileana. The marriage was unhappy and the couple's two youngest children, Ileana and Mir
2 CONC cea, are generally acknowledged to have been sired by Marie's long-time lover, Barbu Știrbey.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferdinand succeeded his uncle on the latter's death (Carol I died without surviving issue) as King of Romania on 10 October 1914, reigning until his own death on 20 July 1927.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the 1,174th Knight of the Order of the Golden Fleece in Austria in 1909 and the 868th Knight of the Order of the Garter in 1924.
2 CONT
2 CONT Further information: Romania during World War I and Diplomatic history of World War I §  Romania
2 CONT Though a member of a cadet branch of Germany's ruling Hohenzollern imperial family, Ferdinand presided over his country's entry into World War I on the side of the Triple Entente powers against the Central Powers on 27 August 1916
2 CONC . Thus he gained the nickname the Loyal, respecting his oath when sworn in before the Romanian Parliament in 1914: "I will reign as a good Romanian."
2 CONT
2 CONT As a consequence of this "betrayal" toward his German roots, Kaiser Wilhelm II had Ferdinand's name erased from the Hohenzollern House register.
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite the setbacks after the entry into war, when Dobruja and Wallachia were occupied by the Central Powers, Romania fought in 1917 and stopped the German advance into Moldavia. When the Bolsheviks sued for peace in 1918, Romani
2 CONC a was surrounded by the Central Powers and forced to conclude the Treaty of Bucharest, 1918. However, Ferdinand refused to sign the treaty. When the Allied forces advanced on the Thessaloniki front, they knocked Bulgaria out of th
2 CONC e war, and Ferdinand ordered the re-mobilization of the Romanian Army. Romania re-entered the war on the side of the Triple Entente.
2 CONT
2 CONT The outcome of Romania's war effort was the union of Bessarabia, Bukovina and Transylvania with the Kingdom of Romania in 1918. Ferdinand became the ruler of a greatly enlarged Romanian state in 1918–1920 following the Entente's v
2 CONC ictory over the Central Powers, a war between the Kingdom of Romania and the Hungarian Soviet Republic, and the civil war in Russia. He was crowned King of Romania in a spectacular ceremony on 15 October 1922 at the courtyard of t
2 CONC he newly opened "Coronation Cathedral" in the historic princely seat of Alba Iulia, in Transylvania.
2 CONT
2 CONT A new period of Romanian history began on the day of the Union of Transylvania with Romania (Great Union Day, Marea Unire). This period would eventually come to an end with the international treaties that led up to World War II. T
2 CONC hese ceded parts of Romania to its neighbors. As such, they are widely seen as an attempt to provoke the country into taking sides and joining the war.
2 CONT
2 CONT Domestic political life during his reign was dominated by the conservative National Liberal party led by the brothers Ion and Vintilă Brătianu. The acquisition of Transylvania ironically enlarged the electoral base of the oppositi
2 CONC on, whose principal parties united in January 1925 – October 1926 to form the National Peasant Party.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferdinand died from cancer in Sinaia in 1927, and was succeeded by his grandson Crown Prince Michael, under a regency. The regency had three members, one of whom was Ferdinand's second son, Prince Nicholas.
