1 NOTE During World War II, when the German army invaded Belgium and France, 
2 CONT Leopold, with the bulk of the Belgian army, was surrounded by the Germans 
2 CONT and quickly capitulated. His surrender incurred the violent disapproval of 
2 CONT the Belgian people and brought accusations of treason. Leopold, however, 
2 CONT refused to administer his country under German control and was imprisoned 
2 CONT first in his castle at Brussels and later in Germany. Despite his defiance 
2 CONT of the Germans, the Belgian government-in-exile in London refused to 
2 CONT recognize his right to rule. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After the liberation of Belgium in 1944, the legislature elected Leopold's 
2 CONT brother Prince Charles as regent. Leopold later went into exile in 
2 CONT Switzerland. In 1946 a commission of inquiry exonerated Leopold of 
2 CONT treason, but the controversy concerning his loyalty continued. He won a 
2 CONT referendum permitting his return, and he returned from exile on July 22, 
2 CONT 1950. Riots soon erupted, and on August 1 he delegated his royal powers to 
2 CONT his son Baudouin. On July 16, 1951, Leopold abdicated in favor of 
2 CONT Baudouin.
1 NOTE KING OF THE BELGIANS (ABDICATED 1951)
