1 NAME Robert Guy Jean Marie /de La Rochefoucauld/
2 GIVN Robert Guy Jean Marie
2 _MARNM de La Rochefoucauld
1 NAME Robert Comte de La Rochefoucauld
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 16 SEP 1923
2 ADDR
3 CITY Paris
1 DEAT
2 DATE 8 MAY 2012
2 PLAC Château de Pont Chevron
2 ADDR
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00342958&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY Robert Guy Jean Marie was born on 16 September 1923 in Paris, the son of Olivier, Comte de La Rochefoucauld, and Consuelo de Maillé de La Tour-Landry. 
2 CONT
2 CONT With his family he lived in a fashionable area near the Eiffel Tower. He attended private schools in Switzerland and Austria, and, at age 15, he received a pat on the cheek from Adolf Hitler on a visit to his Alpine retreat at Ber
2 CONC chtesgaden, according to the British newspaper The Telegraph. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Two year later, Hitler's army invaded France and his father was taken prisoner. Robert became a follower of Charles de Gaulle, who was assembling Free French forces in England. One day a postal worker tipped him off to a letter he
2 CONC  had seen denouncing him to the Gestapo. 
2 CONT
2 CONT With the help of the French resistance, he took a pseudonym and fled to Spain in 1942 with two British airmen who were also sheltered by the underground. He hoped to go on to England and link up with de Gaulle's movement. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Spanish authorities interned the three men, but the British secured their freedom and were so impressed with Robert's resourcefulness that they asked him to join the Special Operations Executive, the clandestine unit known as 
2 CONC the S.O.E., which Prime Minister Winston Churchill created in 1940 to, in his words, 'set Europe ablaze' by working with resistance groups on the German-occupied continent. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert was an asset to the British in another way. As their ambassador in Spain told him, according to The Telegraph: 'The courage and skill of British agents is without equal. It is just that their French accents are appalling.' 
2 CONT
2 CONT The British flew Robert to England to train him to jump out of airplanes, set off explosives and kill a man quickly using only his hands. They parachuted him into France in June 1943. There, he destroyed an electric substation and
2 CONC  blew up railroad tracks at Avallon but was captured and condemned to death by the Nazis. 
2 CONT
2 CONT While being taken for execution, he jumped from the back of his captor's truck, dodged bullets, then ran through nearby streets, winding up outside a German headquarters, where he spotted a limousine flying a swastika flag, its dr
2 CONC iver nearby, the keys in the ignition. He drove off in the car and then caught a train to Paris, hiding in one of its bathrooms. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The S.O.E. later evacuated him to England by submarine, but in May 1944 he parachuted back into France. Dressed as a workman, he smuggled explosives into a huge German munitions plant near Bordeaux, hiding them in hollowed-out loa
2 CONC ves of bread. He set the explosives off on May 20 and fled by bicycle, but was caught by the Germans once more. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In his cell he feigned an epileptic seizure, and when a guard opened the door Robert hit him over the head with a table-leg and then broke his neck. He took the guard's uniform and pistol, shot two other guards, and escaped and co
2 CONC ntacted a French underground worker whose sister was a nun. He donned her habit and walked unobtrusively to the home of a more senior agent, who hid him. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The S.O.E. was disbanded in 1946. As an officer in the postwar French military, Robert trained French troops in the Indochina war and the Suez Campaign, in which the French joined Britain and Israel against Egypt over control of t
2 CONC he Suez Canal. He later pursued international business ventures. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On 8 July 1955 in Diors, Robert married Bernadette de Marcieu de Gontaut-Biron. Bernadette was born on 20 September 1928 in the Château de Selincourt, daughter of Comte Gaston de Marcieu and Gilberte Boudet de Puymaigre. However o
2 CONC n 7 May 1940 she was adopted by Charles Armand de Gontaut-Biron and Madeleine d'Harcourt. Robert and Bernadette became the parents of three daughters and one son. From 1966 to 1996 he was mayor of Ouzouer-sur-Trézée, an idyllic ca
2 CONC nal town in the Loire Valley. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1997 Robert testified on behalf of Maurice Papon, a former official with France's wartime collaborationist Vichy government, who was tried on charge of deporting French Jews who were sent to Nazi death camps. Robert told the co
2 CONC urt that Mr. Papon had risked his life to help the resistance and the Allies. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Papon was convicted of complicity in Nazi crimes against humanity but fled to Switzerland while appealing. He was arrested at a Gstaad hotel, where he had registered as Robert Rochefoucauld. One of Mr. Papon's lawyers said lat
2 CONC er that Robert had given his passport to Mr. Papon. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Maurice Papon was returned to France and served less than three years of his sentence before being released. He died in 2007. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert was a knight in the French Legion of Honor and a recipient of France's Medal of Resistance, and he was decorated for bravery by the British. His memoir 'La Liberté, C'est Mon Plaisir, 1940-1946' was published in 2002. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert, comte de La Rochefoucauld, died on 8 May 2012 in the Château de Pont Chevron. At his death he was believed to have been one of the last living Frenchmen of Churchill's S.O.E.
