1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *Michael Prince(prinz) von Hohenzollern-Sigmaringen.
2 CONT *King of Romania 20 July 1927.
2 CONT *Abdicated as King of Romania, when his father returned to Romania and took over on 8 June 1930.
2 CONT *King of Romania on 6 September 1940.
2 CONT *Knight Grand Cross, Royal Victorian Order (G.C.V.O.). He abdicated as a result of Communist pressure on 30 December 1947.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Links:==
2 CONT
2 CONT *[http://thepeerage.com/p10112.htm#i101115 The Peerage]
2 CONT *[http://www.geneall.net/W/per_page.php?id=6882 Geneall]
2 CONT *[http://www.hansdenyngre.dk/wizg111.htm#1908 Johann the Younger #3577]
2 CONT *[http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michael_I_of_Romania Wikipedia]
2 CONT *[http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Line_of_succession_to_the_Romanian_throne Line of succession to the Romanian throne]
2 CONT *'''King of Romania''' Reign: 20. July 1927–8. June 1930 + 6. September 1940 – 30. December 1947
2 CONT >'''Predecessor:'''[http://www.geni.com/profile/index/6000000000714860723 Ferdinand I] '''Successor:'''/'''Predecessor'''  [http://www.geni.com/profile/index/6000000003193718943 Carol II]
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Michael I (Romanian: Mihai I [miˈhaj]; 25 October 1921 – 5 December 2017) was the last King of Romania, reigning from 20 July 1927 to 8 June 1930 and again from 6 September 1940 until his abdication on 30 December 1947.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly after Michael's birth, his father Prince Carol had become involved in a controversial relationship with Magda Lupescu. In 1925, Carol was eventually pressured to renounce his rights to the throne and moved to Paris in exil
2 CONC e with Lupescu. In 1927, Michael ascended the throne, following the death of his grandfather, Ferdinand I. As he was still a minor, a regency council was instituted which comprised his uncle, Prince Nicholas; the Patriarch Miron C
2 CONC ristea; and the president of the Supreme Court, Gheorghe Buzdugan. The council proved to be ineffective and in 1930, Carol returned to Romania and replaced his son as king, reigning as Carol II. As a result, Michael returned to be
2 CONC ing heir apparent to the throne and was given the additional title of Grand Voievod of Alba-Iulia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Carol II was deposed in 1940, and Michael once again became king. Under the government led by the military dictator Ion Antonescu, Romania became aligned with Nazi Germany. In 1944, Michael participated in a coup against Antonescu
2 CONC , appointed Constantin Sănătescu as his replacement, and subsequently declared an alliance with the Allies. In March 1945, political pressures forced Michael to appoint a pro-Soviet government headed by Petru Groza. From August 19
2 CONC 45 to January 1946, Michael went on a "royal strike" and unsuccessfully tried to oppose Groza's Communist-controlled government by refusing to sign and endorse its decrees. In November, Michael attended the wedding of his cousins,
2 CONC  the future Queen Elizabeth II of the United Kingdom and Prince Philip of Greece in London. Shortly thereafter, on the morning of 30 December 1947, Groza requested a meeting with Michael where he was forced to abdicate. Michael wa
2 CONC s forced into exile, his properties confiscated, and his citizenship stripped. He married Princess Anne of Bourbon-Parma in 1948 with whom he had five daughters and eventually settled in Switzerland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nicolae Ceaușescu's communist dictatorship was overthrown in 1989 and the following year Michael attempted to return to Romania, only to be arrested and forced to leave upon arrival. In 1992, Michael was allowed to visit Romania f
2 CONC or Easter where he was greeted by huge crowds; a speech he gave from his hotel window drew an estimated one million people to Bucharest. Alarmed by Michael's popularity, the government refused to allow him any further visits. In 1
2 CONC 997, after Iliescu's defeat by Emil Constantinescu in the presidential elections of the previous year, Michael's citizenship was restored and he was allowed to visit Romania again. Several confiscated properties, such as Peleş Cas
2 CONC tle and Săvârşin Castle, were eventually returned to his family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents  [hide] 
