1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00083423&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT Married first time with Theresia 22 Apr 1967 	Seefeld    Divorced	1988 
2 CONT
2 CONT Married second time with Theresia 25 Jul 2008 	Murau, Austria 
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karel_Schwarzenberg wikipedia] - '''Karel Schwarzenberg''' (Czech pronunciation: ˈkarɛl ˈʃvartsn̩bɛrk] or Karel, Prince of Schwarzenberg (in German Karl Johannes Nepomuk Josef Norbert Friedrich Antoni
2 CONC us Wratislaw Menas Fürst zu Schwarzenberg), 12th Prince Schwarzenberg (First Majorat) and 7th Prince Schwarzenberg (Second Majorat), Duke of Krumlov born 10 December 1937), is a Czech politician and the leader of TOP 09 party. He 
2 CONC has been Minister of Foreign Affairs of the Czech Republic since July 2010, and he previously held that position from 2007 to 2009. He was originally nominated as Foreign Minister by the Green Party. He served as a Senator from Pr
2 CONC ague from 2004 to 2010. In May 2010, he was elected a member of the Chamber of Deputies, gaining the largest number of preference votes. He was a candidate for President of the Czech Republic in the 2013 presidential election, and
2 CONC  qualified for the second round. He ultimately finished second, receiving 45.19% of the votes. Schwarzenberg is noted for his pro-European views.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Schwarzenberg''' has been the head of the House of Schwarzenberg since 1979. He is a relative of Prince Felix of Schwarzenberg, a leading statesman of the Austrian Empire. From 1948 to 1990, he lived in Austria (where he was kn
2 CONC own as Karl Schwarzenberg), where he was involved in politics for the Austrian People's Party and became a noted critic of human rights violations in the Eastern Bloc, chairing the International Helsinki Federation for Human Right
2 CONC s. Following the fall of communism, he became a close adviser to Václav Havel and relocated to Prague.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is married to '''Therese Hardegg''' ('''Therese Countess zu Hardegg auf Glatz und im Machlande''') and they have three children, who all live in Austria.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Background, education and personal life''':
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Karel Schwarzenberg''' is the eldest son of Prince Karel VI of Schwarzenberg of the junior line (Second Majorat of the House of Schwarzenberg), and Princess Antonie von Fürstenberg. He is also the 1,322nd Knight of the Order of
2 CONC  the Golden Fleece of Austria.[6] He is first cousin of Ira and Egon von Fürstenberg and second cousin of Rainier III, Prince of Monaco. By tradition, he holds the style of Serene Highness (German: Durchlaucht).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Schwarzenberg''' was born in Prague and his family spoke both German and Czech. He is known today for his "slightly archaic and often earthy Czech."[2] The Schwarzenberg family fled the country after the Communist coup d'état i
2 CONC n 1948 and he grew up in Austria.
2 CONT
2 CONT The House of Schwarzenberg originates in Franconia, where the family still owns substantial property[citation needed], but made Bohemia their primary seat in the 17th century, also maintaining residences in Vienna. The family had 
2 CONC possessed fiefdoms in Bohemia as far back as the Middle Ages; it was one of the richest noble families of Bohemia and Austria-Hungary, and one of the largest land owners of Bohemia. Schwarzenberg and his parents had to leave the c
2 CONC ountry after the Communist coup of 1948, and emigrated to Austria, with Swiss citizenship. He studied law and forestry at the universities of Vienna, Munich and Graz.[7] He has two sisters, Marie Eleonore von Bredow (born 1936) an
2 CONC d Anna Maria Freifrau von Haxthausen (born 1946), and one brother, Dr. rer. oec. Friedrich Prinz von und zu Schwarzenberg (born 1940).
2 CONT
2 CONT On 22 April 1967, in Seefeld, Schwarzenberg married Countess Therese zu Hardegg (full name in German according to tradition Therese Gräfin zu Hardegg auf Glatz und im Machlande) (b. Vienna, 17 February 1940). The marriage ended in
2 CONC  divorce in 1988. However, the couple married for the second time on 25 July 2008.[8][9] His wife spends most of her time in Vienna and doesn't speak Czech.[4] The marriage bore three children:[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Johannes Nepomucenus Andreas Heinrich Joseph Karl Ferdinand Johannes Evangelist die Heiligen Drei Könige Achaz Michael Maria, Hereditary Prince of Schwarzenberg (b. Vienna, 13 December 1967), married on 20 March 2010 Diana Orgovan
2 CONC yi-Hanstein, granddaughter of Baroness Maximiliane von Berg and second cousin once removed of Francesca von Habsburg-Lothringen.
2 CONT  Princess Anna Carolina Antoinette Elisabeth Theresia Olga Adelheid Maria of Schwarzenberg (b. Vienna, 16 December 1968), married civilly in London on 28 July 1997 and religiously at Murau on 6 September 1997 Peter Morgan (b. Wimb
2 CONC ledon, London, 10 April 1963), son of Arthur Morgan and wife Inga.
2 CONT  Prince Karl Philipp Ernst Ferdinand Alwig Kilian of Schwarzenberg (b. Vienna, 12 May 1979). Adopted by Austrian industrialist and politician Thomas Prinzhorn (who is, according to some sources, his natural father) by agreement 25
2 CONC  November 1987 and registered in Vienna 16 May 1988. On 20 March 1990 began using surname "Prinzhorn".[10][11][12] Married at Altaussee on 3 October 2009 Countess Anna Elisabeth Aline Henriette Maria Benedikta Stephanie Johanna Li
2 CONC dvine Walpurga Thekla von und zu Eltz (b. Vienna, 25 June 1982).
2 CONT
2 CONT After the fall of the communist regime, Schwarzenberg returned to Prague in 1990, although he still occasionally visits Austria, where part of his family lives. He holds both Swiss and Czech citizenship.[13]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Exile, human rights activism and career in Austrian politics''':
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 1960s, Schwarzenberg was active in the conservative Austrian People's Party and contributed to reforming the party before the 1966 legislative election. Voices inside the party considered him a possible candidate for the po
2 CONC sition of Foreign Minister of Austria, the position he would occupy in the Czech Republic decades later.[14]
2 CONT
2 CONT He soon became active in the resistance against the communist dictatorship in Czechoslovakia and became a prominent human rights advocate, and a leading voice against the communist rule of his native country after the Prague Sprin
2 CONC g. From 1984 to 1991 he was chairman of the International Helsinki Federation for Human Rights, and in 1986 he founded the Dokumentationszentrum zur Förderung der unabhängigen tschechoslowakischen Literatur in Scheinfeld, Germany.
2 CONC  In 1989, he accepted the European Human Rights Prize on behalf of the International Helsinki Federation for Human Rights.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Career in Czech politics''':
2 CONT
2 CONT A longtime friend and close collaborator of Václav Havel, he served as Havel's Chancellor during his tenure as President from July 1990 to July 1992.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Senator''':
2 CONT
2 CONT He served as a Senator in the Czech Senate from the 6th District of Prague from 13 November 2004 to 29 May 2010.
2 CONT
2 CONT While a Senator, he was expelled from Cuba in May 2005 (together with German MP Arnold Vaatz), where he was due to meet dissidents opposed to the Cuban President Fidel Castro.[15]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Foreign Minister of the Czech Republic''':
2 CONT
2 CONT Between 9 January 2007 and 9 May 2009, he was Minister of Foreign Affairs of the Czech Republic in Mirek Topolánek's second coalition government. His nomination by the Green Party caused a small controversy when President Václav K
2 CONC laus stated that he had strong links to Austria and so would not be able to defend national interests.[16][17] On 8 July 2008, he and the American Secretary of State Condoleezza Rice signed an agreement on the United States's Miss
2 CONC ile shield program.[18]
2 CONT
2 CONT In the first half of 2009, Schwarzenberg was also the Council President (Responsible national minister) of the European Union. Also in 2009, he and coalition colleague Miroslav Kalousek formed a new Czech political party TOP 09, w
2 CONC hich they led to success in the general elections of 2010, gaining 16% of the vote and representation in the Czech Parliament.
2 CONT
2 CONT After he left office as Foreign Minister on 8 May 2009, he became Foreign Minister again on 13 July 2010.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Candidate for President of the Czech Republic''':
2 CONT
2 CONT On 11th and 12th January 2013, Schwarzenberg successfully took part in the first round of Czech presidential elections, the first popular vote for presidency of the country. With 23.40% of the total vote, he finished second behind
2 CONC  former prime minister Miloš Zeman (24.21%). Schwarzenberg and Zeman were the remaining two opponents in the second round of this election, held on 25th and 26th January 2013. Karel Schwarzenberg received 45.19% of the votes in th
2 CONC e second round and thus lost to Zeman.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Political positions''':
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Position on communist crimes''':
2 CONT
2 CONT In December 2010, Schwarzenberg, along with the foreign ministers of five other Central and Eastern European EU countries, called upon the European Commission to make "the approval, denial or belittling of communist crimes" an EU-
2 CONC wide criminal offence. Prince Schwarzenberg said that the denial of the crimes of communism is analogous to denying the crimes of Nazism, which in many EU countries is a criminal offense, arguing that "there is a fundamental conce
2 CONC rn here that totalitarian systems be measured by the same standard."[19]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Condemnation of the Beneš decrees''':
2 CONT
2 CONT In January 2013, while running for President of the Czech Republic Schwarzenberg stated that "what we committed in 1945 would today be considered a grave violation of human rights and the Czechoslovak government, along with Presid
2 CONC ent Beneš, would have found themselves in The Hague," referring to the International Criminal Court.[20]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Titles and names''':
2 CONT
2 CONT A member of the high nobility of Bohemia, his full name is '''Karl Johannes Nepomuk Josef Norbert Friedrich Antonius Wratislaw Mena Fürst zu Schwarzenberg''' in German and '''Karel Jan Nepomucký Josef Norbert Bedřich Antonín Vrati
2 CONC slav Menas kníže ze Schwarzenberga''' in Czech. He is generally known as '''Karl zu Schwarzenberg''' in German and uses the Czech form of his first name, Karel, in Czech.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Karel Schwarzenberg''' is the current 12th Prince of Schwarzenberg, through his adoption by Heinrich Schwarzenberg, the brother of Joseph Schwarzenberg, the 11th Prince and last male member of the major Schwarzenberg line (Firs
2 CONC t Majorat).[21] Although Heinrich died before his brother Joseph III. (the 11th Prince), the adoption allowed Karel Schwarzenberg to succeed to the major Schwarzenberg line. Thus, in his person the minor line (Second Majorat), of 
2 CONC which he is part by origin, has been united with the major line.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Czech Republic, using of noble predicates is prohibited by law No. 61/1918 Sb. If Schwarzenberg were to use his titles, his name and style would be:
2 CONT  His Serene Highness The Prince of Schwarzenberg, Count of Sulz, Princely Landgrave in Klettgau, and Duke of Krumlov.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Awards''':
2 CONT  In 1989, he received together with Lech Wałęsa, later president of Poland, the Council of Europe Human Rights Prize.
2 CONT  In 2003, he received the Order of Tomáš Garrigue Masaryk 3rd Class of the Czech Republic.
2 CONT  He has also been a Knight of the Order of the Golden Fleece (Austrian branch) since 1991.
2 CONT  In 2005, he received the Decoration for Services to the Republic of Austria in Silver with Sash.
2 CONT  In 2008, he received the Grand Cross of the Federal Cross of Merit – Bundesverdienstkreuz – of the Federal Republic of Germany.
2 CONT
2 CONT Schwarzenberg is also a regular attendant to the Trilateral Commission and the Bilderberg meetings.
2 CONT
